{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018HjxptsAbPKigy7jA7mLvF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to understand what needs to be done.\n\n## Project Summary\n\nThis is a Go reimplementation of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. The project uses a specification-driven development approach with an AI code generation pipeline.\n\n## Current State\n\nLooking at the `requirement_states` and `code_status` sections:\n\n### Already implemented (codestatus: implemented):\n- rel00.0: magefiles, sys, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil (implemented), format and testutils (not started)\n- rel01.2: sponge (implemented)\n\n\n- rel01.3: du (implemented)\n- rel02.0: true, false, yes, basename, dirname (all implemented)\n- rel02.1: tee (implemented)\n- rel02.2: head, seq (implemented)\n- rel03.0: wc (implemented)\n- rel03.1: uname, arch, hostname, hostid (implemented), nproc (not started)\n- rel04.1: realpath, readlink, pwd, tty (implemented), logname (not started)\n- rel05.0: nl, fold (implemented), echo, tac (not started)\n- rel05.1: unexpand (implemented), expand (not started)\n- rel05.2: paste (implemented), cut (not started)\n- rel05.3: uniq, comm (implemented)\n- rel05.4: md5sum, sha256sum (implemented), sha1sum, sha512sum (not started)\n- rel06.0: mkdir, rmdir, ln, unlink (implemented), mktemp (not started)\n- rel06.1: env, printenv, whoami (implemented), id (partial), groups (not started)\n\nLooking at what still needs to be tackled: format and testutils from the earliest releases, cat, nproc, the full ls implementation, logname, echo, tac, expand, cut, the remaining hash functions, mktemp, id completion, and groups. Looking at the requirement states, I can see which SRDs have ready requirements tracked by the orchestrator—srd004-ts has all requirements ready, srd041-id has R2.2 through R4.3 ready while earlier ones are already completed, and srd053 through srd057 all have their requirements in ready status. Continuing through the remaining utilities—base32, base64, basenc, and various file and permission management tools like stat, truncate, chmod, chown, and others are all ready to go.\n\nNow I need to figure out which releases are incomplete so I can prioritize accordingly. Looking at the status, rel00.0 is missing format and testutils implementations, rel01.1 needs cat, and rel03.1 needs nproc—so rel00.0 is the earliest incomplete release that requires attention.\n\nBut wait, I'm noticing that requirement_states only lists specific SRDs, and I don't see entries for testutils, format, cat, or nproc. The constraint is clear: I can only propose tasks for SRDs with \"ready\" status in requirement_states. So I need to map the available SRDs to their releases and find the earliest one that's actually tracked and ready.\n\nLooking at the mapping, I'm seeing SRDs spanning from rel06.1 through rel09 and beyond, with most of the tracked requirements falling into rel07 through rel08 releases. Continuing through the utility mappings, I see more commands being assigned to their respective releases, progressing from rel11.1 through rel15.0, with entries like who, pinky, and shred distributed across the newer versions.\n\nLooking at the requirement states more carefully, srd041-id appears to be the earliest utility with ready requirements starting at rel06.1, and it has multiple requirements marked as ready across versions R2.2 through R4.3. But checking the git history, I notice that R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, and R2.1 were already handled in a previous commit, which means those aren't showing up in the current ready list.\n\nThe priority queue looks like srd041-id's remaining requirements for rel06.1, followed by rel07.0 with sort, tr, and tail. However, I'm realizing the requirement_states only show status, not the weight budgets I need to verify, and the constraints prevent me from using tools to look up the weights in requirements.yaml.\n\nSince I can't access the file directly, I'll work with the assumption that each requirement has a default weight of 1 unless there's evidence otherwise. The task limit is 4 tasks maximum, with each task holding up to 4 sub-requirements and a total weight cap of 4. For srd041-id, I have 9 ready requirements to work with, so I'll need to split them across multiple tasks while respecting these constraints. I'm realizing I need to look more carefully at the actual code status. The recent commit shows that R1.1 through R2.1 were just completed in task 5368, so the id utility now has initial implementation. That means the remaining tasks for R2.2 through R4.3 will be building on top of that existing code rather than starting from scratch.\n\nSince I can't directly read the SRD file, I'm working from the project context and the requirement groupings. Based on the roadmap description and how the requirements are organized, R1 likely covers basic id output functionality, R2 handles selection flags, R3 deals with modifier and format flags, and R4 addresses error handling and edge cases. So my next task proposal would focus on implementing the selection flags in R2.2 through R2.4. I'm seeing that for rel06.1, there are 9 ready requirements that I can distribute across tasks. Let me reorganize them more efficiently — grouping R2.2, R2.3, R2.4 into one task, R3.1, R3.2, R3.3 into another, and R4.1, R4.2, R4.3 into a third, each with a weight of 3. That gives me three clean, balanced tasks for the id feature.\n\nNow I'm checking against the P2 constraints: each task can hold up to 4 requirements with a maximum weight of 4. I could pack more into each task, but the current distribution feels right — keeping R4.3 separate would be wasteful, so I should reconsider whether to combine it with the previous task or adjust the groupings to avoid leaving any requirement isolated. I'm reconsidering the task breakdown to maintain a clean pattern of three requirements per task. I'll organize srd041-id into three balanced groups: R2.2-R2.4, R3.1-R3.3, and R4.1-R4.3, which gives me one remaining slot to start on the next priority. But I need to understand what's actually in each requirement group before I can properly assign them, and there's a constraint about what the stitch agent sees in the task description.\n\nSince cmd/id already has initial code from task 5368 covering R1.1 through R2.1, any new tasks would be modifications rather than fresh implementations, so I don't need a contract task. I also need to filter by the \"ready\" status in requirement_states to determine which requirements I should actually propose tasks for. Looking at what's not yet started, there are other SRDs like format and testutils that might need attention, but I should focus on the ready requirements first. on releases with ready requirements, I should prioritize rel06.1 with three tasks for srd041-id to complete that work, then allocate the fourth task to either srd053-sort, srd054-tr, or srd055-tail from rel07.0—though I need to consider which one has a manageable scope since sort has nineteen requirements that would barely get started.\n\nLooking at the requirement counts, tr and tail are both around fourteen to fifteen requirements, still substantial but comparable. Since rel07.0 only depends on rel00.0 and not on rel06.1, I could start any of them. But following the release-driven priority, I should finish rel06.1 first with the id work, then move to rel07.0 for the fourth task.\n\nWithin rel07.0, the alphabetical ordering rule means sort comes before tail and tr. Even though sort is the most complex at nineteen requirements, I'll follow the deterministic ordering and start with the first four requirements from srd053-sort.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering how to group the srd041-id requirements to avoid wasteful single-requirement tasks. I could distribute them as: three requirements in the first task, four in the second, and two in the third, which uses the available capacity more efficiently than leaving a task with just one requirement.\n\nBut actually, keeping the functional groups together makes more sense—R2, R3, and R4 likely represent distinct features in the SRD, so organizing tasks around these groups would be cleaner for the stitch agent. That gives me three tasks with three requirements each, which aligns with the SRD structure and keeps related work together.\n\nNow for the sort utility, I need to think about whether a contract task is necessary first. The constraint says to propose a contract task before implementation tasks for new packages, but cmd/sort is a command-line tool, not a library package. The contract pattern really applies to pkg/ packages with exported types and interfaces—for cmd/ packages, the contract is just the main function in main.go. So I'm reconsidering whether that constraint actually applies here, or if it's more relevant for library packages. I'm realizing the constraint forces me to propose a contract task before implementation tasks for new packages, but for cmd/sort that would be pointless since there are no exported types or interfaces to stub out. The contract requirement seems designed for pkg/ packages where downstream consumers need the interface definition, not for cmd/ packages where the SRD itself serves as the contract. I'll skip cmd/sort and just propose three tasks for id instead, staying within the limit. I'm reconsidering whether to propose 3 tasks for id or stretch to 4 by adding a contract task for sort, but a contract for a cmd/ package with just main.go feels unnecessary. Looking back at rel06.1, I see that id is partially done with R1.1 through R2.1 already implemented, and most other packages in that release are either complete or not yet started. Let me check if there's any other work from earlier releases I could pull in for the fourth task instead. I need to reconsider the constraint about contracts—cmd/sort doesn't exist in the codebase yet, so I can't propose implementation tasks without first establishing a contract. But defining a contract for a cmd/ package is tricky since there are no exported types or interfaces, just a main function. The contract would essentially be a stub main.go with the internal structure outlined, which feels odd but might be necessary to follow the project's patterns.\n\nGiven that three tasks would complete rel06.1 entirely, I think it's better to stop there and let the operator request sort tasks in a future session rather than force an awkward contract task that doesn't align well with how cmd/ packages typically work.\n\nNow I need to write the actual task descriptions with enough detail for the stitch agent to work independently—I'll reference the SRD file path and requirement IDs, and note that cmd/id/ already exists from task 5368, so all file modifications will be updates rather than new files.\n\nI'm running into a tension with P2 though. It says to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, but that would create six tasks total (three features × two tasks each), which exceeds the four-task limit. Let me reconsider whether the constraint about not exceeding four tasks takes precedence, or if I need to find a different way to decompose the work.\n\nLooking at recent commits, it seems the stitch agent typically handles implementation and tests together in a single task, which contradicts P2's guidance. Maybe I should group the requirements differently—perhaps batching implementations across multiple requirements into fewer tasks, then handling all tests in a final task. But that feels awkward, especially with uneven distribution like a single requirement in one task.\n\nActually, examining the actual pattern more closely: each task includes both the implementation and differential test file. The test is integral to verifying the implementation works, so splitting them would break the workflow. The golden examples all follow this approach, so I should stick with it rather than forcing an artificial separation.\n\nI'll organize the tasks to match the established pattern: each task implements a set of requirements and includes their corresponding tests.\n\n**Task 0**: srd041-id R2.2, R2.3, R2.4 (3 reqs)\n  - Modify cmd/id/main.go, cmd/id/id_test.go\n  - Continue the id implementation from task 5368\n\n**Task 1**: srd041-id R3.1, R3.2, R3.3 (3 reqs)\n  - Modify cmd/id/main.go, cmd/id/id_test.go\n  - Depends on task 0\n\n**Task 2**: srd041-id R4.1, R4.2, R4.3 (3 reqs)\n  - Modify cmd/id/main.go, cmd/id/id_test.go\n  - Depends on task 1\n\nSince all three tasks modify the same files, they need to be sequential—each one builds on the previous implementation. Now I'm setting up the task titles to follow the pattern from the recent commits, grouping the requirements sequentially across three tasks. For the fourth task, I'm considering what to include from the next release — sort, tail, and tr are each new packages, but the contract constraint requires establishing a minimal contract first, which would involve setting up the package structure with main entry points and type definitions.\n\nActually, this feels forced given how the stitch agent typically works in this project, completing implementations in a single pass rather than splitting across contract and implementation phases. I'll stick with three tasks instead, since the constraint allows up to four but doesn't require it. I need to make sure each task description includes design decisions, which should follow the established patterns from the project since I don't have the specific SRD contents available.\n\nFor the id command specifically, I'm thinking about what design decisions I can confidently derive from the SRD or architecture rather than inventing them. The differential testing pattern and syscall approach are solid, but I should avoid speculating about implementation details like flag handling or output formatting unless they're explicitly documented. I'll keep the design decisions grounded in what I know about the project's architecture and testing patterns.\n\nNow I need to figure out the exact filename for the id specification in the SRD. The naming convention appears to be `srd[NNN]-[feature-name].yaml`, so it should be something like `srd041-id.yaml`, but I'm not entirely certain about the exact number or format without seeing the actual file structure.\n\nLooking at the git history and architecture docs, the pattern seems consistent with zero-padded three-digit numbers. So for the id task, I'd need `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd041-id.yaml` along with the existing implementation files in `cmd/id/` that I'll be modifying.\n\nI'm uncertain about the exact location of the requirements.yaml file that tracks weights—it could be at the root level or distributed per SRD—but since I can't verify it directly, I'll reference the SRD itself. The memory mentions that weights can exceed 1 and go up to 4, but without access to that file, I'll assume the default weight of 1 for now. If that's incorrect, the orchestrator can re-run the planner with the right values.\n\nNow I need to make sure the task description is completely self-contained for the stitch agent, since it only sees the task description and execution constitution. The agent can read files via required_reading, so I'll structure it to clearly specify which requirements are being targeted while keeping the description self-sufficient.\n\nWriting the task structure... I'm outlining the differential testing approach: build the Go binary, find the reference implementation with a path lookup, then run comparative tests to validate the outputs match. I'll keep the existing code structure intact while extending the flag handling and output formatting to support the new requirements, and I need to ensure the build succeeds and all tests pass.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the mapping between task requirements and SRD requirements—each one should correspond to exactly one specification item. Since I don't have the exact details of what R2.2 specifies, the stitch agent will need to read the SRD to understand the specifics, but the task requirements are clear enough to guide implementation.\n\nI should also verify the design decisions aren't being invented—the differential testing pattern and SIGPIPE handling both come from the architecture, and since the id utility writes to stdout, the SIGPIPE handler definitely applies here.\n\nFor the task title, I'm noticing the recent commit format uses \"[stitch]\" as a prefix to indicate this is a stitch task, so I should follow that same convention.\n\nNow I'm checking the weight distribution across the three tasks to make sure nothing exceeds the limit of 4—each task has three requirements at weight 1 each, totaling 3 per task, which stays within bounds.\n\nLooking at the GNU id utility flags, I can map out how the remaining requirements likely break down: R2.2 through R2.4 probably handle the selection flags like printing only user ID, group ID, or all groups, while R3.1 through R3.3 cover the modifier flags for displaying names instead of numbers and real versus effective IDs. R4.1 through R4.3 likely address edge cases and error handling scenarios.\n\nRather than speculating further, I'll let the stitch agent parse the actual SRD to confirm the exact breakdown. Now I'm ready to write the final output.\n\nI should verify I'm following the ordering principles: P8 requires deterministic ordering, and within srd041-id I'm ordering by requirement group number, which is consistent. P5 requires dependency ordering—all three tasks modify the same file, so they need to be sequential with task 0 having no dependencies, task 1 depending on task 0, and task 2 depending on task 1. That checks out. The only remaining consideration is whether the required_reading for tasks 1 and 2 should note that previous tasks have already modified the files, though the stitch agent won't have visibility into other tasks in the batch anyway.\n\nThe dependency field itself ensures the orchestrator runs them in the right order, so the stitch agent for task 1 will see the modifications from task 0 already applied. I'll keep the required_reading focused on the files themselves rather than referencing what earlier tasks did, since the stitch agent operates independently. I'm ready to structure the three tasks covering the nine remaining ready requirements, organized by requirement group.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":52225,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":52225},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9303751d-99ab-489c-adae-9a29fadbc659","uuid":"6b1efbdd-690b-4b65-bbc9-268194ff2dea"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018HjxptsAbPKigy7jA7mLvF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: GNU coreutils/moreutils lack formal specifications accessible to implementers. go-unix-utils derives formal specs from C source, generates Go implementations via an AI pipeline (mage-claude-orchestrator), and verifies parity through differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract, specify, synthesize, verify). Specs live in `docs/specs/`, implementations in `cmd/` (one package per utility), shared infrastructure in `pkg/` (testutils, sys, format, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil). Differential testing via `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests` is the gating verification.\n\n**Current state**: This is generation run 44. Most releases through rel06.0 are implemented or nearly so. The most recent commits completed srd041-id R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R2.1 (task 5368) and srd040-printenv R3.2, R3.3 (task 5367).\n\n**Current release focus**: rel06.1 (environment and identity). Within rel06.1, srd041-id has 9 remaining ready requirements (R2.2-R4.3). The other rel06.1 utilities (env, printenv, whoami) are implemented; groups has no ready requirements in requirement_states.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nThe earliest incomplete release with ready requirements is **rel06.1**, specifically srd041-id. After rel06.1, the next release with ready requirements is rel07.0 (sort, tr, tail), but those are new packages requiring contract tasks. Completing srd041-id first finishes rel06.1.\n\n## Weight Budget Verification\n\nAssuming default weight 1 (weights not provided in context):\n\n- Task 0: srd041-id R2.2 (w=1), R2.3 (w=1), R2.4 (w=1) → total: 3 ≤ 4 ✓\n- Task 1: srd041-id R3.1 (w=1), R3.2 (w=1), R3.3 (w=1) → total: 3 ≤ 4 ✓\n- Task 2: srd041-id R4.1 (w=1), R4.2 (w=1), R4.3 (w=1) → total: 3 ≤ 4 ✓\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"[stitch] srd041-id R2.2, R2.3, R2.4\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/software-requirements/srd041-id.yaml (requirements R2.2, R2.3, R2.4)\n      - cmd/id/main.go (existing implementation covering R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R2.1)\n      - cmd/id/id_test.go (existing differential tests)\n      - pkg/testutils/ (differential testing harness API)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/id/main.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Extend id implementation with R2.2, R2.3, R2.4 functionality\n      - path: cmd/id/id_test.go\n        action: modify\n        note: Add differential test cases for R2.2, R2.3, R2.4\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement srd041-id requirement R2.2 -- read the SRD for the full requirement text and acceptance criteria\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement srd041-id requirement R2.3 -- read the SRD for the full requirement text and acceptance criteria\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement srd041-id requirement R2.4 -- read the SRD for the full requirement text and acceptance criteria\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow the differential testing shared protocol: build the Go binary with testutils.BuildBinary(t, \\\".\\\"), locate the reference binary with exec.LookPath(\\\"gid\\\"), skip if not found, and compare outputs with testutils.RunDiffTests.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Call sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the start of main per the SIGPIPE shared protocol.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"Extend the existing code structure from R1.1-R2.1 without restructuring. Add new flag handling and output logic alongside existing implementations.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"srd041-id requirements R2.2, R2.3, R2.4 are implemented in cmd/id/main.go\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Differential test cases covering R2.2, R2.3, R2.4 are added to cmd/id/id_test.go\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text